.TH MCPD 8 "September 2026" "linux-mcp-daemon" "System Administration Commands"
.SH NAME
mcpd \- Linux Model Context Protocol Daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mcpd
.RB [ \-\-config\-dir
.IR DIR ]
.br
.B mcpd \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mcpd
is a background daemon designed to securely bridge AI agents directly with the Linux operating system using the Model Context Protocol (MCP). It allows AI agents to introspect and interact with the host system via raw syscalls and the Virtual File System (/proc, /sys).
.PP
The daemon listens on a configurable port (default 9091) for HTTP Server-Sent Events (SSE) and JSON-RPC messages. It enforces rate limiting and requires Bearer token authentication.
.SH CONFIGURATION
.B mcpd
reads its configuration upon startup from the directory given by \fB\-\-config\-dir\fR, else \fB$MCPD_CONFIG_DIR\fR (the container image sets \fB/etc/mcpd/configs\fR), else \fBconfigs/\fR under its working directory (the systemd unit's \fB/etc/mcpd\fR): \fBdaemon.yaml\fR (server port, TLS, rate limits, worker timeouts), \fBusers.yaml\fR (users and salted token hashes) and \fBmcp-sudo.yaml\fR (which tools each user may run as root).
.PP
To apply changes without a restart, a user granted \fBdaemon/reload-config\fR calls that tool (\fBlinuxctl reload daemon\fR). The files are validated first - strictly, so an unknown key is an error - and an invalid file leaves the running configuration untouched. Server settings (port, TLS, worker.containerized) still need a restart.
.PP
Logging goes to standard error through log/slog, configured by the \fBlogging\fR block of daemon.yaml: \fBlevel\fR (error, warn, info, debug), \fBformat\fR (text or json) and \fBaccess_log\fR. Audit lines for calls that change the host, and config reloads, are written at any level.
.PP
TLS is on by default: HTTPS on port 9091 with the certificate and key in \fBserver.tls\fR; with \fBgenerate: true\fR a missing pair is created (self-signed) on startup and its SHA-256 fingerprint logged, for clients to pin (\fBlinuxctl describe mcpd tls\fR shows it). Plain HTTP (\fBserver.http\fR) is off by default.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MCPD_CONFIG_DIR
The config directory, unless \fB\-\-config\-dir\fR is given. \fBlinuxctl\fR's \fBmcpd\fR commands use it as their default \fB\-\-config\-path\fR too.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I configs/daemon.yaml
Server, TLS, rate limits and worker settings.
.TP
.I configs/users.yaml
Users and their salted token hashes (mode 0600). Configurations from before this file listed users in daemon.yaml; that still works until linuxctl moves them here.
.TP
.I configs/mcp-sudo.yaml
Rules defining which users are allowed to execute which tools with root privileges.
.SH SECURITY
.B mcpd
runs as the root user in order to bind to network interfaces and inspect the system. However, it spawns unprivileged ephemeral worker processes to handle non-administrative tasks, enforcing strict privilege separation. Directory traversal attacks and other malicious inputs are actively filtered.
.SH AUTHOR
Antigravity
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR linuxctl (1)
